# Independently authored LISTEN / UNLISTEN / NOTIFY
# MySQL-wire-path smoke for the GPL MTR-format runner.
# NOT copied or adapted from MySQL's mysql-test/.
#
# Existing coverage (do NOT duplicate):
#   - LISTEN / UNLISTEN / NOTIFY — NONE in the compat-mtr
#     corpus (checked all *.test files per PR #357 lesson).
#   - sleep_stub_basic_extra, name_const_basic_extra,
#     unsupported_fn_sentinels_basic_extra cover FN-level
#     stubs; this is a STATEMENT-level stub for the MySQL
#     wire path only.
#
# This slice pins (MYSQL WIRE PATH ONLY):
#   - LISTEN <channel> parses and silently succeeds.
#   - UNLISTEN <channel> and UNLISTEN * (wildcard) parse
#     and silently succeed.
#   - NOTIFY <channel> parses and silently succeeds.
#   - NOTIFY <channel>, '<payload>' parses and silently
#     succeeds.
#   - Statements produce no rows (MTR runner emits only
#     the echo line for no-row statements).
#
# CRITICAL framing (corrected from PR #366 review):
#   This slice pins ONLY what the MTR runner observes
#   over MySQL wire. The behavior DIFFERS by wire path:
#
#   - **PGWIRE clients** (psql, libpq, pg-jdbc):
#     LISTEN/NOTIFY are FULLY IMPLEMENTED. The pgwire
#     handler at zeta-pgwire/src/handler.rs:1492-1517
#     intercepts these statements BEFORE the executor
#     and calls real handlers (handle_listen,
#     handle_unlisten, handle_notify). Session-tracked
#     `listening_channels`, buffered `pending_notifications`,
#     transactional flush at COMMIT (line 1791),
#     ROLLBACK-discard (line 1814), wildcard UNLISTEN.
#     See docs/listen_notify_guide.md for the documented
#     PG-compatible feature.
#
#   - **MYSQL WIRE clients** (mysql, mysqlsh, jdbc-mysql,
#     and THIS MTR runner): NO interception. zeta-
#     mysqlwire/src/*.rs has zero handling of these
#     statements. They route to the executor's catch-all
#     at lib.rs:14581-14583 which returns
#     `Ok(ExecuteResult::Ok)` with no I/O. So MySQL-wire
#     clients SEE silent success but never receive
#     events.
#
#   This wire-path divergence is the slice's central
#   pin. It's NOT a "zeta-doesn't-support-pub/sub"
#   claim — that would be false.
#
# Big-picture finding (WIRE-PATH-DEPENDENT LIMITATION):
#   Apps using MySQL wire that try LISTEN/NOTIFY will see
#   silent success but no events delivered. The lack of
#   error makes the limitation INVISIBLE to apps relying
#   on it. Three remediation paths:
#
#     1. Use pgwire instead of MySQL wire (the canonical
#        fix — full LISTEN/NOTIFY works there).
#     2. Use the CDC/logical-replication path
#        (pg_recvlogical / Debezium) consuming the log
#        stream — works for any wire client.
#     3. Roll a polling-table pattern on a shared table.
#
#   STRIKING — there is no way from inside MySQL-wire
#   SQL to detect that LISTEN/NOTIFY isn't backed by a
#   real pub/sub layer. Apps need out-of-band knowledge
#   of the wire path or the docs note in
#   docs/listen_notify_guide.md.
#
# Adaptation notes:
#   # zeta-adapt: Over MYSQL WIRE only, LISTEN / UNLISTEN /
#     NOTIFY are SILENT NO-OPS. Over PGWIRE these
#     statements are FULLY IMPLEMENTED — choose your
#     wire path accordingly.
#   # zeta-adapt: For MySQL-wire pub/sub, use CDC
#     (pg_recvlogical / Debezium) or a polling-table
#     pattern; LISTEN/NOTIFY won't deliver events.
#   # zeta-adapt: See docs/listen_notify_guide.md for
#     the pgwire-path feature documentation.

--echo === MySQL wire path — LISTEN parses and produces no rows ===

LISTEN cache_invalidate;
LISTEN job_queue;
# Quoted name with spaces.
LISTEN "with spaces";

--echo === MySQL wire path — UNLISTEN parses (named and wildcard) ===

UNLISTEN cache_invalidate;
UNLISTEN job_queue;
UNLISTEN *;

--echo === MySQL wire path — NOTIFY (bare) parses and produces no rows ===

NOTIFY cache_invalidate;

--echo === MySQL wire path — NOTIFY with payload string ===

NOTIFY cache_invalidate, 'cache_key_42';
NOTIFY cache_invalidate, 'payload with spaces';
NOTIFY cache_invalidate, '';

--echo === MySQL wire path — sequence LISTEN→NOTIFY→UNLISTEN all silent ===

# Over pgwire this would: subscribe → queue an event →
# unsubscribe. Over MySQL wire it's three no-ops.
LISTEN test_seq;
NOTIFY test_seq, 'hello';
UNLISTEN test_seq;

--echo === Sanity — subsequent SELECT shows no rows leaked from above ===

SELECT 1 AS sanity;
